diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported outage on it_lab3. The host’s L2/L3 stack is healthy: eth0 is UP with link detected (ethtool shows link yes, 10Gb/s, full duplex), it has the correct IP 192.168.1.5/24 and default route via 192.168.1.1. From it_lab3 I successfully pinged its gateway (router0, 192.168.1.1) with 0% loss, and router0 can ping back it_lab3 with 0% loss. ARP on it_lab3 shows a REACHABLE neighbor entry for 192.168.1.1, and router0’s ARP table shows it_lab3’s MAC reachable. Intra-VLAN and inter-VLAN reachability are also good: it_lab3 can ping it_lab1 (192.168.1.3), office_pc (192.168.3.2), and an Internet-segment host pc2 (128.168.0.2), all with 0% loss and reasonable RTTs.

Affected component: none; all interfaces, addressing, and routing are operating normally.
Mechanism: Successful ARP resolution and ICMP across L2/L3 paths demonstrates the port, NIC, and gateway adjacency are fine.
Localization: Verified on it_lab3 eth0 and router0 eth0 (192.168.1.0/24 segment).
Root cause: Not reproducible—no persistent switch-port, NIC, or routing fault is present at this time.
Corrective fix: No changes required. it_lab3 is currently on a working port and fully reachable. If the issue recurs, capture “ip addr/route,” “ip neigh,” and a ping to 192.168.1.1 while the problem is occurring, and consider reseating the cable or trying a different switch access port to rule out an intermittent physical-layer issue.
=== END ===